}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne \\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139547118 17592186576411 false] [17592186511507 1074 "We say that three vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith 